<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#persian-language">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Persian language</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Persian is an Iranian language spoken mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and Tajikistan, whose literary form developed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Islamic conquest</a> from earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a>. It became a prestige language of administration and culture across much of the Islamic world. Persian traditions passed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> into the Abbasid state, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> used Persian as its language of government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/persian-language.jpg" alt="Persian language" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jahongard · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Farsi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Modern Persian is a continuation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a>, an official language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> (224–651 CE), which was itself a continuation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a>, used in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> (550–330 BCE). It originated in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> in southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Old Persian is attested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> inscriptions from the 6th to the 4th century BCE, the oldest being the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription</a> from the time of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> (reigned 522–486 BCE). Middle Persian is attested in Pahlavi and Manichaean scripts, and New Persian literature was first recorded in the ninth century, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-conquest-of-persia">Muslim conquest of Persia</a>. Gernot Windfuhr treats New Persian as an evolution of both earlier stages, but states that none of the known Middle Persian dialects is its direct predecessor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Persian belongs to the Western Iranian group of the Iranian languages. It has three standard varieties: Iranian Persian, Dari in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and Tajiki in Tajikistan, all based on classic Persian literature and mutually intelligible to a relatively high degree. Iranian Persian and Dari are written in the Persian alphabet, a modified form of the Arabic alphabet; after the Arab conquest it took about 200 years before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> adopted the Arabic script in place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a>. Tajiki is written in a Cyrillic alphabet.</p><p>Modern Persian has no grammatical gender and its usual word order is subject–object–verb. Its core vocabulary is of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> origin, but it also contains a considerable number of Arabic loanwords. John R. Perry estimates that about 20 percent of everyday vocabulary, and around 25 percent of that of classical and modern literature, is of Arabic origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Persian was the first language to break the monopoly of Arabic on writing in the Muslim world, and Persian poetry became a tradition in many eastern courts. It served as a language of bureaucracy even for non-native speakers, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtuns</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. It influenced Turkic, Armenian, Georgian, Greek and Indo-Aryan languages, as well as other Iranian ones. Works written in it include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdowsi">Ferdowsi</a>, the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a>, the Rubaiyat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omar-khayyam">Omar Khayyam</a>, the Divân of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez">Hafez</a> and the Gulistan and Bustan by Saadi Shirazi. Approximately 130 million people speak it worldwide.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Persian language” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Copied, illustrated and recited for a thousand years, it shaped the <b>Persian language</b> and the self-image of rulers from Anatolia to Central Asia.</blockquote><a href="/work/shahnameh">Shahnameh · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
